Indexes - 
An index is a data structure that improves the speed of data retrieval operations on a table.

Types of Indexes

1. Primary Index (Clustered Index)

Created automatically on the Primary Key in many databases.

2. Secondary Index (Non-Clustered Index)

Created on non-primary-key columns.

3. Composite Index

Index on multiple columns.

Advantages of Indexes - Faster SELECT Queries , Faster Sorting, Faster Joins
Disadvantages of Indexes - Extra Storage, Slower INSERT, Slower UPDATE, Slower DELETE

When Should You Create an Index?

Good candidates:

Frequently searched columns
Primary keys
Foreign keys
Columns used in JOIN
Columns used in WHERE clauses
Columns used in ORDER BY


When Should You Avoid Indexes?

Bad candidates:

Small tables
Columns rarely queried
Columns with frequent updates
Columns with very few unique values (sometimes)

An index is a database data structure that improves query performance by allowing faster data retrieval. Most databases implement indexes using B-Trees. Indexes speed up SELECT, JOIN, and ORDER BY operations but increase storage usage and can slow INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations because the index must also be maintained.

Indexes(done) → Clustered vs Non-Clustered → Transactions → Isolation Levels → Deadlocks

