Locks - 
A lock is a concurrency control mechanism that restricts access to a database resource while a transaction is using it.

Types of Locks - 

1. Shared Lock (S Lock)

Also called Read Lock.

Multiple transactions can read simultaneously.
No transaction can modify the locked data.

2. Exclusive Lock (X Lock)

Also called Write Lock.

Transaction can read and modify data.
No other transaction can read or write the locked resource.

Row-Level Lock

Lock only specific rows.

Locks are concurrency control mechanisms used to ensure data consistency when multiple transactions access the same data simultaneously. The two main types are Shared Locks, which allow multiple reads, and Exclusive Locks, which allow a single transaction to modify data while blocking others. Locks help maintain isolation and prevent issues such as lost updates and dirty reads.

