Concurrency problems - 
Concurrency problems occur when multiple transactions access and modify the same data simultaneously, leading to inconsistent or incorrect results.

1. Dirty Read

A dirty read occurs when a transaction reads uncommitted data from another transaction.

2. Non-Repeatable Read

A non-repeatable read occurs when a transaction reads the same row multiple times and gets different values because another transaction modified and committed the row.

3. Phantom Read

A phantom read occurs when a transaction re-executes a query and finds rows that were inserted or deleted by another committed transaction.

4. Lost Update

A lost update occurs when concurrent transactions overwrite each other's changes.

5. Dirty Write

One transaction overwrites another transaction's uncommitted data.

Concurrency problems occur when multiple transactions access the same data simultaneously. Common issues include Dirty Reads, where uncommitted data is read; Non-Repeatable Reads, where the same row returns different values; Phantom Reads, where query results contain new or deleted rows; and Lost Updates, where one transaction overwrites another transaction's changes. These problems are controlled using locks and isolation levels.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Isolation levels - 
Isolation Levels define how much one transaction is isolated from other concurrent transactions.

1. Read Uncommitted

The lowest isolation level.

Transactions can read uncommitted data from other transactions.

Allows Dirty Read, Non-Repeatable Read, Phantom Read

2. Read Committed

Most commonly used isolation level in many databases.

A transaction can only read committed data.

Prevents Dirty Read
Allows Non-Repeatable Read, Phantom Read

3. Repeatable Read

Ensures that rows read during a transaction cannot change until the transaction completes.
Prevents Dirty Read, Non-Repeatable Read

Allows Phantom Read (theoretical SQL standard answer)

4. Serializable

Highest isolation level.

Transactions execute as if they run one after another.

Prevents Dirty Read, Non-Repeatable Read, Phantom Read

Isolation levels define how transactions interact with each other when running concurrently. The four standard isolation levels are Read Uncommitted, Read Committed, Repeatable Read, and Serializable. As the isolation level increases, data consistency improves and concurrency problems are reduced, but performance and concurrency generally decrease.

