DBMS VS RDBMS  - 

DBMS is software used to manage databases, whereas RDBMS is a type of DBMS that stores data in relational tables and maintains relationships using primary and foreign keys while supporting ACID properties and normalization.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Primary Key VS Unique Key - 

Primary Key

A Primary Key uniquely identifies each row in a table.

Properties
Must be unique
Cannot be NULL
Only one Primary Key per table
Automatically creates an index

Unique Key

A Unique Key also ensures that values are unique.

Properties
Must be unique
NULL values are allowed (database-dependent)
Multiple Unique Keys can exist in a table
Creates an index

Both Primary Key and Unique Key enforce uniqueness. The main difference is that a table can have only one Primary Key and it cannot contain NULL values, whereas a table can have multiple Unique Keys and they generally allow NULL values. A Primary Key is used as the main identifier for records, while Unique Keys enforce uniqueness on other columns such as email or phone number.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Super key vs candidate key

A Super Key is any set of attributes that uniquely identifies a row, whereas a Candidate Key is a minimal Super Key with no redundant attributes. Every Candidate Key is a Super Key, but not every Super Key is a Candidate Key.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is Denormalization?

Denormalization is the process of adding redundant data or combining tables in a normalized database to improve query performance.
